const chapterRows=[
 ['There Is No One Left','Cholera empties Mary’s Indian household, leaving the neglected child suddenly without parents or servants.'],['Mistress Mary Quite Contrary','At a clergyman’s bungalow and then under Mrs Medlock’s charge, Mary learns she will be sent to a reclusive uncle in Yorkshire.'],['Across the Moor','A train and night carriage carry Mary across the wind-beaten moor to immense, dark Misselthwaite Manor.'],['Martha','Martha’s frankness, stories of Dickon, and description of the locked garden begin to redirect Mary’s attention outward.'],['The Cry in the Corridor','Fresh air, the robin, and Ben Weatherstaff stir Mary’s interest while a distant cry suggests the house keeps another secret.'],['There Was Someone Crying—There Was!','Rain drives Mary through forbidden corridors until Mrs Medlock abruptly ends her search for the crying voice.'],['The Key in the Garden','After Martha brings a skipping rope, Mary finds an old key in earth disturbed beside the garden wall.'],['The Robin Who Showed the Way','The robin exposes the hidden door beneath ivy, and Mary enters the abandoned garden alone.'],['The Strangest House Anyone Ever Lived In','Mary clears weeds, asks Martha for tools and seeds, and begins treating the hidden garden as living work.'],['Dickon','Dickon arrives with creatures and tools; his knowledge and trust turn Mary’s secret into a friendship.'],['The Nest of the Missel Thrush','Mary and Dickon work carefully around new shoots and decide the garden must remain protected like a nest.'],['Might I Have a Bit of Earth?','Mary asks her uncle for earth to cultivate; Archibald grants freedom in the grounds and then leaves again.'],['I Am Colin','Following the cry at night, Mary discovers Colin and learns the manor hides a second lonely, commanding child.'],['A Young Rajah','Mary returns to Colin, speaks honestly about illness and the garden, and resists the habits of obedience surrounding him.'],['Nest Building','Dickon’s letter and the robin’s nesting deepen Mary and Colin’s desire to bring him—and eventually Colin—into the secret.'],['I Won’t! Said Mary','Colin demands Mary’s attendance; she refuses, and their quarrel exposes how alike their neglected tempers are.'],['A Tantrum','Mary confronts Colin’s night terror, examines the feared lump on his back, and breaks the cycle of panic.'],['Tha’ Munnot Waste No Time','Dickon visits Colin with his animals; Mary finally reveals that the garden is real and promises to take Colin there.'],['It Has Come!','Dr Craven finds Colin altered by hope, while preparations begin for a carefully concealed excursion outdoors.'],['I Shall Live Forever—and Ever—and Ever!','Colin enters the secret garden and turns his first day among growing things into a vow to live.'],['Ben Weatherstaff','Ben discovers the children over the wall; Colin stands to answer his disbelief and recruits him into the secret.'],['When the Sun Went Down','Colin walks between Mary and Dickon, and the garden becomes the stage for strength practiced away from watching adults.'],['Magic','The children name growth, will, and companionship “Magic,” then begin a daily ritual of confident experiment.'],['Let Them Laugh','Colin hides his gains at meals while Mrs Sowerby’s visit brings adult tenderness into the children’s secret world.'],['The Curtain','Colin draws back his mother’s portrait and claims continuity with her rather than the fatal future he feared.'],['It’s Mother!','Susan writes to Archibald; in Italy he responds to memory, dream, and the invitation to return to the garden.'],['In the Garden','Archibald returns, finds Colin walking, and crosses the lawn beside his son as the household witnesses restoration.']]
const timelineId=id('timeline','main')
const chapters=chapterRows.map(([title,summary],i)=>({...base,id:Ch(i+1),timelineId,number:i+1,title,summary,status:'final',targetWordCount:null}))

const beats=[
 [1,'epidemic','The Silent Bungalow','The cholera outbreak scatters the household and Mary is forgotten in the nursery.','india',{mary:'Waits angrily for service until silence makes her realize that ordinary authority has vanished.',mrs_lennox:'Recognizes the danger too late as illness overtakes the household.',ayah:'Dies during the epidemic, leaving Mary without the caregiver she assumed would always obey her.'},4],[1,'found','Officers Find Mary','British officers enter the abandoned bungalow and discover Mary alive and alone.','india',{mary:'Answers the astonished officers without grief she knows how to express.'},3],
 [2,'clergyman','Among the Crawford Children','Mary stays briefly with a clergyman’s noisy family and earns the rhyme “Mistress Mary Quite Contrary.”','india',{mary:'Rejects the children’s attempts to include her and clings to solitary command.'},2],[2,'medlock-charge','Mrs Medlock Takes Charge','Mrs Medlock tells Mary about Archibald Craven and the isolated Yorkshire house.','bombay',{mary:'Listens for the first time to the mystery of her uncle, his grief, and his hundred rooms.',medlock:'Accepts responsibility for delivering Mary to Misselthwaite and warns her not to expect attention.'},2],
 [3,'arrival-station','North to Thwaite','Train travel ends at a small Yorkshire station where a carriage waits in the rain.','station',{mary:'Arrives tired but alert to the unfamiliar accents and cold.',medlock:'Moves the journey efficiently from railway to estate carriage.'},2],[3,'moor-crossing','Across the Moor at Night','The carriage crosses darkness, wind, and open moor before reaching the manor.','front-drive',{mary:'Feels the moor as a vast dark sea and meets the house with apprehensive curiosity.',medlock:'Interprets the moor and manor while keeping Mary close through the final approach.'},3],
 [4,'martha-morning','Martha Opens the Curtains','Martha refuses servile deference and describes the moor and her large family.','mary-room',{mary:'Is offended by equality yet fascinated by Martha’s warmth and independent speech.',martha:'Treats Mary as a capable child and speaks proudly of Dickon and home.'},2],[4,'garden-story','The Locked Garden','Martha explains that Mrs Craven’s garden has been locked since her death.','mary-room',{mary:'Fixes on the garden as the first mystery that belongs to her rather than to the adults.',martha:'Shares the old household story without realizing how fully it will occupy Mary.'},2],
 [5,'ben-robin','Ben and the Robin','Outdoors, Mary meets Ben Weatherstaff and the robin who lives near the walled gardens.','kitchen-garden',{mary:'Makes an awkward first attempt at friendly conversation.',ben:'Recognizes another sour, lonely creature and grudgingly allows her company.'},2],[5,'first-cry','A Cry Behind the Walls','At night Mary hears a child crying somewhere beyond the corridor.','corridor',{mary:'Refuses to accept the servants’ explanation that wind made the unmistakably human sound.',martha:'Tries to redirect Mary before curiosity threatens the household secret.'},3],
 [6,'rain-exploration','Portraits and Closed Rooms','Rain confines Mary indoors, where she explores unused rooms and old portraits.','corridor',{mary:'Turns boredom into deliberate trespass and begins mapping the forbidden house.'},2],[6,'medlock-interrupts','Caught in the Corridor','Mrs Medlock finds Mary near the source of the cry and sends her sharply back.','corridor',{mary:'Understands from the housekeeper’s alarm that someone truly is hidden.',medlock:'Protects Colin’s seclusion by ending Mary’s search and tightening the rule against wandering.'},3],
 [7,'rope-gift','A Rope from Mrs Sowerby','Martha gives Mary a skipping rope purchased by her mother.','mary-room',{mary:'Receives her first thoughtful gift and begins practicing rather than ordering someone else to act.',martha:'Delivers Susan’s practical kindness and encourages Mary outdoors.'},1],[7,'key-found','The Buried Key','Newly turned earth near the wall reveals the key hidden ten years earlier.','wall-walk',{mary:'Connects the object to the locked garden and hides it with purposeful excitement.'},3],
 [8,'robin-clue','The Robin at the Ivy','The robin’s movements draw Mary to a loose curtain of ivy on the wall.','secret-door',{mary:'Reads the bird’s behavior as a clue and uncovers the long-concealed door.',ben:'Watches from a distance without realizing what the robin has shown her.'},3],[8,'first-entry','Inside the Secret Garden','Mary unlocks the door and steps into the rose garden sealed since Lilias died.','secret-garden',{mary:'Claims responsibility for protecting the place and immediately searches for signs that its plants remain alive.'},4],
 [9,'first-weeding','Clearing Around the Shoots','Mary pulls grass carefully from emerging bulbs and old rose roots.','secret-garden',{mary:'Works patiently for the sake of living things whose needs cannot be commanded.'},2],[9,'letter-to-dickon','A Request for Tools','Mary asks Martha to write to Dickon for child-sized tools and seeds.','mary-room',{mary:'Risks sharing part of her purpose because the garden needs knowledge and equipment.',martha:'Recognizes Mary’s changed appetite and gladly carries the request to Dickon.'},2],
 [10,'dickon-arrives','Dickon Among the Creatures','Dickon arrives with a fox, crow, seeds, and gardening tools.','secret-garden',{mary:'Trusts Dickon with the hidden entrance after seeing how animals trust him.',dickon:'Studies the neglected garden gently and promises to help without exposing it.'},3],[10,'garden-partners','The First Shared Work','Mary and Dickon prune, loosen earth, and identify the living roses.','secret-garden',{mary:'Works beside a friend and learns to ask rather than demand.',dickon:'Teaches through demonstration while preserving Mary’s ownership of the discovery.'},2],
 [11,'nest-secret','A Garden Like a Nest','The children compare their secrecy to the missel thrush protecting its nest.','rose-bower',{mary:'Accepts secrecy as care rather than possession alone.',dickon:'Balances enthusiasm with caution so the garden can recover undisturbed.'},2],[11,'spring-signs','Roots and Green Points','More bulbs and rose shoots show that the ten-year neglect did not kill the garden.','secret-garden',{mary:'Begins measuring days by growth and feels her own strength increase with the work.',dickon:'Identifies which wood is alive and which growth needs space and light.'},2],
 [12,'uncle-interview','Mary Meets Her Uncle','Archibald summons Mary before leaving and asks what she wants.','archibald-room',{mary:'Asks simply for a bit of earth, protecting the garden without lying about her need.',archibald:'Is startled by Mary’s resemblance to Lilias and grants her freedom to cultivate the grounds.'},3],[12,'freedom-granted','Freedom of the Grounds','Mary tells Martha that her uncle has allowed her to go anywhere outside.','mary-room',{mary:'Understands the permission as both protection for the garden and trust she must use well.',martha:'Celebrates the request as evidence that Mary now wants something healthy and real.'},1],
 [13,'cry-followed','The Cry Found','During a storm Mary follows the voice through the corridor to a lighted room.','corridor',{mary:'Pushes past fear and prohibition because the hidden child matters more than rules.'},4],[13,'meet-colin','I Am Colin','Mary and Colin discover they are cousins and compare their secluded lives.','colin-room',{mary:'Speaks with direct curiosity instead of the fearful obedience Colin expects.',colin:'Finds an equal-aged visitor who is neither servant nor doctor and keeps her beside him.'},3],
 [14,'return-to-colin','Two Contrary Children','Mary returns and challenges Colin’s certainty that he will die and develop a crooked back.','colin-room',{mary:'Counters rehearsed fear with blunt observation and refuses to flatter him.',colin:'Alternates command, fascination, and anger as Mary offers a future he has not permitted himself.'},3],[14,'garden-imagined','The Garden Described','Without admitting entry, Mary describes what a hidden spring garden might contain.','colin-room',{mary:'Uses the garden’s real details to hold Colin’s attention and calm his fear.',colin:'Begins wanting an outdoor place strongly enough to imagine surviving to see it.'},2],
 [15,'dickon-letter','Dickon’s Picture Letter','A drawing of a missel thrush assures Mary that Dickon is guarding the secret.','mary-room',{mary:'Reads the picture as proof of trust and decides Colin may eventually deserve the same confidence.',martha:'Brings the message and notices how completely Mary’s days now have purpose.'},1],[15,'colin-wants-dickon','Colin Wants to Meet Dickon','Mary’s accounts of animals and moorland make Colin eager for Dickon’s visit.','colin-room',{mary:'Tests Colin’s capacity to welcome someone he cannot command by rank.',colin:'Desires companionship and the outdoor world more strongly than his familiar sickroom drama.'},2],
 [16,'summoned','Colin Sends for Mary','Colin orders Mary to abandon Dickon and attend him immediately.','secret-garden',{mary:'Chooses her work and friendship over submission to Colin’s demand.',dickon:'Stays out of the cousins’ quarrel while affirming that Mary may choose for herself.'},3],[16,'quarrel','I Won’t','Mary and Colin exchange furious truths about selfishness, illness, and control.','colin-room',{mary:'Refuses banishment and names Colin’s tyranny because she recognizes her former self in it.',colin:'Meets resistance that wounds his pride but also breaks the obedience sustaining his isolation.'},4],
 [17,'night-tantrum','The Midnight Scream','Colin’s terror erupts through the manor until Mary enters his room.','colin-room',{mary:'Converts fear of the screaming into anger strong enough to confront him.',colin:'Believes pain and a lump on his back prove death is near and loses control.',medlock:'Cannot calm Colin and reluctantly allows Mary to attempt what the adults fear.'},5],[17,'back-examined','No Lump at All','Mary examines Colin’s back and makes him see that the dreaded deformity is not there.','colin-room',{mary:'Uses plain evidence and ridicule to interrupt the story of inevitable decline.',colin:'Stops screaming, accepts the physical evidence, and allows hope to replace immediate panic.'},3],
 [18,'dickon-visits','Dickon Brings the Moor Indoors','Dickon enters Colin’s room with animals and news of spring.','colin-room',{mary:'Watches her two friendships join without losing either one.',colin:'Responds with delight instead of command and questions Dickon about living things.',dickon:'Treats Colin naturally while letting animals and stories carry the visit.'},2],[18,'truth-revealed','Mary Reveals the Garden','Mary admits that she found the garden and promises Colin will enter it.','colin-room',{mary:'Entrusts Colin with the secret after he promises not to endanger it.',colin:'Turns the garden from fantasy into a concrete future and begins planning to leave the room.'},4],
 [19,'doctor-surprised','Dr Craven Finds Laughter','The doctor arrives expecting collapse and finds Colin sitting up with Mary.','colin-room',{colin:'Announces that fresh air with Mary will help him and controls the interview without hysteria.',mary:'Supports the plan while preventing medical caution from reviving fear.',dr_craven:'Observes a striking change and agrees to an outing despite uncertainty.'},2],[19,'outing-plan','Preparing the Secret Excursion','The children arrange wheelchair, servants, and a route that will conceal the garden entrance.','colin-room',{mary:'Coordinates the practical deception needed to protect the garden.',colin:'Uses his household authority toward a hopeful goal rather than a sickroom performance.',dickon:'Checks the path and weather so Colin’s first journey can be safe.'},3],
 [20,'garden-entry','Colin Enters the Garden','The chair passes through the ivy door and Colin sees his mother’s garden in spring.','secret-garden',{mary:'Shares the discovery freely and watches Colin receive what first belonged only to her.',colin:'Experiences sunlight, scent, and enclosure as evidence of life rather than danger.',dickon:'Guides the chair gently and lets the garden itself command attention.'},5],[20,'vow-to-live','A Vow Beneath the Roses','Colin declares he will recover and return to the garden every day.','rose-bower',{mary:'Accepts Colin’s determination as a shared project instead of another boast.',colin:'Replaces certainty of death with an equally fierce intention to live and study his strength.',dickon:'Grounds the vow in food, rest, fresh air, and repeated practice.'},4],
 [21,'ben-over-wall','Ben Weatherstaff Appears','Ben looks over the wall and is shocked to find the forbidden garden occupied.','secret-garden',{mary:'Defends the children’s work and refuses to let surprise become accusation.',colin:'Confronts Ben’s pity and the rumor that he is a cripple.',dickon:'Keeps the animals calm while the old gardener recognizes Lilias’s garden.',ben:'Sees Colin for the first time and realizes the garden has been restored in secret.'},4],[21,'colin-stands','Colin Stands','Supported only at first, Colin rises and stands before Ben.','tree-seat',{colin:'Turns anger at disbelief into the first public proof of his strength.',mary:'Counts and encourages without disguising the effort required.',dickon:'Positions himself close enough to help while letting Colin own the act.',ben:'Drops his guarded manner and joins the secret in astonished loyalty.'},5],
 [22,'walking-practice','Between Mary and Dickon','Colin practices walking a few steps while Ben watches the garden entrance.','secret-garden',{mary:'Matches Colin’s pace and makes daily practice ordinary rather than miraculous.',colin:'Walks farther while learning to stop before exhaustion.',dickon:'Supports balance and keeps attention on the path rather than fear.',ben:'Guards the secret and begins contributing his long knowledge of the roses.'},4],[22,'sunset-rest','When the Sun Went Down','The children rest at sunset after work, food, and exercise.','rose-bower',{mary:'Recognizes that her happiness now depends on shared routines and other people’s growth.',colin:'Feels tired without interpreting fatigue as illness.',dickon:'Reads the evening weather and sends the others home before cold settles.'},2],
 [23,'magic-circle','Naming the Magic','Colin calls the force in growth, thought, and companionship “Magic.”','fountain',{mary:'Joins the ceremony because its promise reflects changes she has already lived.',colin:'Organizes his imagination around recovery and repeated action.',dickon:'Accepts Colin’s language while understanding the practical natural processes beneath it.',ben:'Participates despite embarrassment because he sees the children’s work succeeding.'},3],[23,'scientific-plan','The Great Scientific Experiment','Colin decides to record exercise, appetite, and growth as an experiment.','secret-garden',{mary:'Helps turn declaration into a daily plan with honest measurements.',colin:'Combines imagination with observation and begins expecting evidence from himself.',dickon:'Supplies outdoor tasks calibrated to Colin’s increasing strength.'},2],
 [24,'household-deception','Let Them Laugh','Colin pretends to remain frail at meals so his eventual recovery will be undeniable.','dining',{colin:'Enjoys controlling the secret but increasingly eats and acts for health rather than attention.',mary:'Finds the performance comic while ensuring Colin still eats enough.',medlock:'Mistakes the children’s private laughter for another eccentric phase but notices calmer days.'},2],[24,'susan-visits','Mrs Sowerby in the Garden','Susan enters the garden, feeds the children, and recognizes what their companionship has accomplished.','secret-garden',{mary:'Receives maternal warmth without suspicion and sees how care can include correction.',colin:'Calls Susan mother-like and accepts affection without turning it into command.',dickon:'Welcomes his mother into the secret he has helped tend.',susan:'Sees three undernourished forms of loneliness healing through work, food, and friendship.'},4],
 [25,'portrait-uncovered','The Curtain Drawn Back','Colin uncovers Lilias’s portrait and studies the mother he has been taught to associate with catastrophe.','colin-room',{colin:'Claims resemblance and memory as sources of belonging rather than guilt.',mary:'Helps make the portrait part of the present instead of a forbidden relic.'},3],[25,'walks-indoors','Walking the Corridor','Colin secretly practices beyond his room while the household believes he remains in the chair.','corridor',{colin:'Carries the garden’s confidence into the house and lengthens his unaided walk.',mary:'Keeps pace and checks that the route stays clear of servants.',dickon:'Waits at the turning point and steadies Colin only when balance truly fails.'},4],
 [26,'susan-letter','Susan Writes to Archibald','After judging the time right, Susan sends Archibald a plain invitation to come home.','cottage',{susan:'Acts as the adult willing to connect the children’s hidden recovery with their absent father.'},2],[26,'archibald-dream','A Voice in the Italian Garden','At Lake Como, Archibald dreams of Lilias calling him to the garden and finds Susan’s letter the next morning.','italy',{archibald:'Feels the first clear desire to return and chooses the journey home instead of continued flight.',lilias:'Lives in Archibald’s dream and memory as an invitation toward love rather than grief.'},4],
 [27,'father-returns','Archibald Finds the Open Door','Archibald reaches the grounds, hears children, and enters the garden he ordered locked.','secret-door',{archibald:'Crosses the threshold expecting memory and instead finds his son alive and moving.',colin:'Runs toward his father, ready to reveal the recovery he has rehearsed in secret.',mary:'Steps back from the reunion while recognizing that opening the garden changes the whole house.'},5],[27,'walk-home','Father and Son Cross the Lawn','Colin walks beside Archibald toward the manor as servants gather in disbelief.','front-drive',{archibald:'Returns to the house beside his living son and accepts a future no longer organized by loss.',colin:'Shows the household his strength without chair or concealment.',mary:'Watches the secret work become shared restoration rather than a private refuge.',medlock:'Sees Colin walking and understands that the locked rooms and old rules no longer define the family.'},5],
]
